# Names through the book

Much of what an author does with names already reaches across the book. A table-of-contents entry names a chapter by its title, wherever the chapter lives. Go-to-definition, find-references, and rename work over the whole corpus, so renaming a section rewrites every loom that mentions it.

The anchor's own lookup reaches across the book too. An anchor finds a section in another loom by the section's name, never by the path of the loom it lives in, so the author never has to know which file a chapter is kept in. That also frees the parentheses after an anchor for the chapter "Referring to modules", which lets them name a file the book emits.

This chapter changes the lookup so that a name resolves by its place in the book: in its own chapter first, then among the chapters, and by a path of names for the rest. The lookup stays where it is today, in the corpus, and no new pass runs before compiling. The book's names and references, seen together, form a graph, and that graph is derived from the corpus whenever something wants to draw it.

## Where a name lives

The corpus is a tree of named things. It holds one book or several, as the chapter "Books" describes. A book holds parts. A part holds chapters, and it may also hold discussions, the looms the chapter "Discussions" describes. A chapter holds its sections. A section holds the sections nested under its heading and the boxes written in it. The tree is also the scope. It decides which names must differ, and which names a reference can see.

A name must be unique among its siblings, and nowhere else. Two chapters in one part may not share a title, and neither may two sections of one loom, or two boxes in one section. Two parts may each hold a chapter called "Setup". Twenty-seven chapters of the book already hold a section called "What this module draws on", and that stays legal.

Every section of a loom is a sibling of every other, whatever level its heading stands at. A section's name is its identity within its loom: the editor names the section's composed code by it, and the compiler finds a section by it. So a section titled like the chapter heading above it repeats a name, and so does a pair of titles that fold to one identifier, such as `Saying hello` and `Saying Hello!`.

A duplicate among siblings is a `DuplicateName` fault at the name that repeats it, because no reference could ever tell the two apart.

The table of contents reads the same scope. An entry finds its chapter by the part it is listed under and by its title, so two "Setup" chapters in two parts each answer to their own entry. With books, the book a contents section belongs to joins the part and the title.

## Finding a name from where it stands

A reference resolves nearest first, outward from the section it stands in. A bare name, `::[Resolving names]`, looks first among the sections and boxes of its own chapter. When none matches, it looks among the chapters and discussions of its own book. A bare name never finds a part or a book, and never a chapter of another book.

Every bare anchor in the book keeps its meaning under this rule. A chapter is one loom, so a bare name still looks in its own loom first, as it does today.

A box of kind `Chapter` or `Discussion` stands for the loom it points to. A name that finds the box finds that loom once it exists, so a reference written beside the box resolves before and after the loom is opened.

An author writes a name that is out of reach as a path. A path lists names from the outside in, separated by commas, and each name after the first lies inside the one before it. `::[The multi-file AST, Resolving names]` names a section of another chapter. `::[The shape of a loom, The multi-file AST, Resolving names]` names the same section through its part. A book or a part enters a reference only this way, at the head of a path. A path into another book starts with the book's name, as `::[The website, Part I, Chapter 2, Pricing]` does, and the part comes next.

A path may also name a part or a chapter by its number, the way a loom's identity line does. `::[Part II, Chapter 6, Resolving names]` is the short form of the full path above. Each name in a path takes either form, so `::[Part II, The multi-file AST, Resolving names]` names the same section too, and a discussion takes its number as `Discussion 1`. A section has no number, so a path that ends at a section always ends with the section's title.

A number names a position, not a chapter. When a chapter moves, a numbered reference points at whichever chapter took the number, and it still resolves, so no fault reveals the change. Two things guard against it. Renumbering a chapter through the editor rewrites every numbered reference to it in the same edit. And the editor shows the chapter's title beside each numbered reference, so a reader sees where the reference points.

A bare name is ambiguous when it matches two things at the nearest scope where it matches at all. The fault sits at the reference, not at either declaration, since both are legal where they stand. The `AmbiguousAnchor` fault lists the paths that would each resolve, and the editor offers every one of them as a fix. Two parts may each hold a chapter called "Setup", and nothing is wrong until a bare `::[Setup]` asks for one of them.

A term reference, written `::[Account]{Term}`, has a lookup of its own. It looks only among terms, through the chain of glossaries that the chapter "The Glossary trope" describes, and a plain anchor never finds a term. A term and a section may therefore share a name without making a reference to either one ambiguous.

## The lookup, changed in place

The corpus already holds everything the rule reads. The `titleIndexes` function indexes each loom's sections by title, and `chapterIndex` indexes the book's chapters by the titles their frontmatter declares. The frontmatter also carries each chapter's part numeral and chapter number, which index the short form. So `resolveAnchor` keeps its place and changes its steps. It looks in the anchor's own loom through `titleIndexes`. Failing that, it looks for a chapter among every chapter of the book, each kept with its part. For a path, it finds the chapter that the path's leading names give, by title or by number, and walks the rest through that chapter's titles.

The navigation functions, the weave, and the `anchorEdges` function behind `reachable` and `dependentsOf` already resolve through `resolveAnchor`, so they follow the new rule at once.

The product pass sees one loom at a time. It binds a bare name that its own loom holds, as it does today. A name its loom does not hold becomes an edge that names the section but not yet its loom, the way `crossFileRef` already makes an edge the pass cannot judge alone. The compiler binds that edge through `resolveAnchor` once it holds the corpus, and `crossFileFaults` judges it as it judges every edge into another loom.

Loom reads no loom's path in an anchor. The product pass faults an anchor that still writes a target in parentheses after its name, with the `RetiredTarget` fault, whose one fix removes the target.

## To be settled

A literate author writes a reference before its target as often as after it, because the prose follows the reader's order and not the compiler's. Programming meets the same need with a mock, which fakes a piece's behaviour so the rest of a program can run, or with a typed hole, which marks a gap the compiler can describe. Literate programming has long allowed a looser order. Knuth's WEB names its sections in reading order, so a section is often used before it is written, and the tools object only to a name that is used and never defined.

Loom lets an author carve a program into named compartments first and write each one later. A compartment that holds no code yet is unsettled, and it takes one of three shapes.

The first is an anchor that promises a section nobody has written. The `{To be settled}` label promises it, as `::[Greeting function]{To be settled}` does. So do the names the section will provide, as `::[Greeting function]{provides=greet}` states them, since a section meant to provide `greet` is meant. Either promise says the name is meant, so the anchor raises no fault, and neither asks for prose before the author is ready to specify the section. The editor offers the label while the author types between an anchor's braces.

The second is a section declared with its heading and the prose that specifies it, but no code yet. Its prose already says what its code will do, so it is the literate counterpart of a mock. It fakes no behaviour, but it states the intent, and a reader can read it before any code exists. A box that points to a chapter not yet written leaves a whole loom to be settled the same way.

The third is a wildcard, a box that holds the place of a compartment before the author knows where its code will live. The section "A wildcard holds a place" describes it.

So a reference that composes or refers names one of three things:

- **Something missing.** No node in reach carries the name, and the anchor promises none: it carries no `{To be settled}` label and states no `provides`. It is a typo, or a section not yet declared. The `UnresolvedAnchor` fault reports it. The editor offers two fixes: one declares the section, a heading below the section that names it, and the other labels the anchor `{To be settled}`.
- **Something unsettled.** The anchor promises its section, with the label or with `provides`, and its name finds nothing, or the node exists but holds no code yet: a section with prose alone, a box whose chapter is not written, a wildcard that points nowhere, or a chapter that emits no file yet. The reference resolves, and nothing is wrong while the author writes. The `UnsettledSection` note marks the reference in the editor, the graph of the book lists every unsettled reference, and the woven page marks each one, the way the website marks a page it has not written yet as "Being written".
- **Something written.** The node holds code, and the reference composes it or refers to it. When the node belongs to a canvas in another chapter, a composing reference also raises the warning the chapter "Canvases" describes.

Being unsettled belongs to the reference, not to the section. Many sections hold prose alone by design, such as the opening section of most chapters. A section with no code becomes unsettled only when a composing anchor names it.

A chapter named whole is the exception. A composing anchor that names a chapter reaches the chapter's opening section. When that section holds no code, the `ChapterWithoutCode` warning tells the author that the anchor composes nothing. A chapter with no section at all leaves the anchor nothing to reach, so the `UnresolvedAnchor` fault reports it.

A section is settled once it holds code. An anchor labelled `{To be settled}` whose section has since been written keeps a label with nothing left to say, so the `SettledLabel` note marks it, with a fix that removes the label.

An unsettled reference can be read, but not shipped. The tangler refuses a file whose composition reaches one, and it names each one it met, a chapter named whole whose opening section holds no code among them. Without that rule, the anchor would compose nothing, and the tangled file would carry a gap that nothing points to.

## A contract stated on the anchor

An anchor may state the contract itself, before any code uses the section it reaches. Its `provides` parameter lists the names the anchor expects that section to provide, in the grammar the chapter "Parameters in braces" gives a specifier. One name stands alone, as in `::[Greeting function]{provides=greet}`, and several form a list, as in `::[Greeting function]{provides=[greet, farewell]}`.

The parameter is optional. Many anchors transclude a fragment that declares nothing, such as a run of imports, a piece of an expression, or the lines of a configuration file. Such an anchor has no names to state, so it leaves the parameter out.

What a target provides follows the kind of edge. A composing anchor draws the section into the scope of the section that holds it, so the section provides the names it declares in that scope. A referring anchor, one of the `Reference` kind, names a module where the code expects a module path, as the chapter "Referring to modules" describes. It names a file the book emits, so the file provides the names it exports. One parameter carries both meanings, and the edge decides which one applies. An import usually lists the names it takes, as `import { roomBuilder }` does, and the language service already checks those. So a referring anchor needs `provides` mainly in a language whose import names only the module, such as Python's `import room`.

Loom checks the contract without knowing the language. A language server calls each name a document declares a symbol. It reports every symbol with its name and its kind, such as a function or a class, through the protocol's `textDocument/documentSymbol` request. Loom asks the service that owns the section, or the emitted file, for those symbols and compares their names with the names the anchor states. A section's names are the outermost symbols its own code declares, so a method inside a class is not among them, and neither is a name that a section it composes declares. A stated name that the target does not provide is the `MissingSymbol` fault at the anchor. The editor runs the check, since the language services run there. A tangle runs none, so it never raises `MissingSymbol`, just as it never raises a type error.

Stating the names promises the section, so `::[Greeting function]{provides=greet}` needs no label beside them. While the section is unsettled, the stated names join its contract, which the section "The contract an unsettled section collects" describes.

## Using a name before it is written

The code around an unsettled section goes on using the names the section will provide. A call to `greet("world")` follows `::[Greeting function]{provides=greet}` long before anyone writes `greet`. The language service finds no such name, and it reports an error that tells the author nothing new.

While the section is unsettled, the language package lowers that error to a warning, and the warning names the promise: "`greet` is promised by the section `Greeting function`, which is not written yet." Each language package knows which of its own diagnostics say that a name is missing. TypeScript reports 2304 and 2552 for a name it cannot find, and 2305 for a name a module does not export. Loom gives the package the promised names of each file. They are the names an anchor or a wildcard states in its `provides` parameter, and every name the code imports from a chapter that emits no file yet.

The rule is narrow. It lowers a diagnostic only when the diagnostic names a promised name, only in a file that composes the unsettled section or refers to it, and only while that section is unsettled. Once the section is written, a missing name is an error again, beside the `MissingSymbol` fault at the anchor that promised it.

Nothing is composed in the anchor's place, so the code the language service checks is the author's own. The price is what the service cannot check yet. A call that passes `greet` the wrong arguments goes unnoticed until the section is written, because nothing yet says what `greet` takes. A tangle lowers nothing, since it refuses a composition that is not settled.

## A wildcard holds a place

An author often knows what a program needs before knowing where the code will live. The app needs a greeting that provides `greet` and `hello`, but the author has not yet decided whether the greeting becomes a section of this chapter or a chapter of its own. A wildcard names that need. It is a box of kind `Wildcard`, the block the chapter "Boxes" defines. Code reaches the wildcard by the wildcard's name. The `provides` parameter lists the names the wildcard promises, and the body says in prose what the need is for.

```
## The app

The app greets its user before it does anything else.

::: Greeting module {kind=Wildcard provides=[greet, hello]}
The words the app shows when it starts.
:::

=>
import greet from ::[Greeting module]{Reference}
```

The author settles the wildcard by anchoring it to a section or a chapter, in this loom or another. The `anchors` parameter names the target:

```
::: Greeting module {kind=Wildcard provides=[greet, hello] anchors=Greeting}
The words the app shows when it starts.
:::
```

The value of the `anchors` parameter is a name, and it resolves like any reference, nearest first, by the rule this chapter sets. A name with a space is quoted, as in `anchors="Room's 2d system"`, and a path names a target that a bare name cannot reach. Go-to-definition follows the name, and renaming the target rewrites it.

Every anchor to the wildcard stays as it was. An anchor finds the wildcard by the wildcard's name, and a wildcard with a target behaves exactly as its target does. The anchor composes the target section or refers to the target's file, as the anchor's own kind decides: an anchor of the `Reference` kind refers, and any other composes. Settling a wildcard therefore rewrites no reference to it.

The wildcard keeps its contract after it is settled. Loom checks its `provides` names against the target, as it checks an anchor's, so a name the target lacks is the `MissingSymbol` fault at the wildcard. The wildcard then reads as the interface its target meets.

A wildcard with no target is unsettled, and so is one whose target holds no code yet. Whether the anchor refers decides what an unsettled wildcard gives the code, by the rule the chapter "Referring to modules" sets:

- **As a reference,** the anchor refers to a module that the editor serves in the wildcard's place. The language package writes the module, and the module exports each promised name and a default, so `import greet from` and `import { greet, hello } from` both resolve. TypeScript's package gives each export the type `any`.
- **As a composing anchor,** it composes nothing, and each promised name the code uses warns, as the section "Using a name before it is written" describes.

Only the editor serves a wildcard's module. A tangle refuses an unsettled wildcard, as it refuses every unsettled reference, so the module never reaches a tangled file.

An anchor's own promise stays the short form. A `{To be settled}` label or a `provides` parameter on one anchor needs no box. A wildcard earns its box when several anchors share one promise, or when the need deserves a name and a sentence of its own.

## The contract an unsettled section collects

An unsettled section holds no code, but the code that uses it already says much about it. Each use shows how a name is meant: the arguments a function receives, the members an object must have, the type a value must fit. Those uses, with the names that anchors and wildcards promise, form the unsettled section's contract. The code that depends on the section writes it.

Loom gathers the contract across the book, not within one file. The anchors and the wildcards give the names. The language service gives the uses, since it type-checks the code around each one. An import from a chapter that emits no file yet reaches an empty module, which the editor hands the service in place of the file. The service then reports each name the code asks of that module.

The editor offers the contract's names as completions. It offers them in the code that uses the section, and in the section itself while the author writes it, until the section declares each one.

A stub is a section written from its contract, as far as a machine can write one. Loom writes no stubs. A trope writes them as an assist, one of the contributions the chapter "Tropes" describes. Editors have long offered to create a function or a class from its uses. They read each call site, take each parameter's type from the argument passed, and write a declaration whose body is the language's own placeholder. TypeScript's placeholder throws an error, Rust's calls `todo!()`, and Python's raises `NotImplementedError`. A trope does the same from the contract Loom gathers, so a language whose service infers nothing from uses still gets a stub with the right names.

The contract also says where the stub belongs, which no editor knows alone. The stub goes into the unsettled chapter, as a new section with a heading for its name and an empty specification for the author to write.

A stub is code, so the section it lands in is settled. The language's placeholder is what still marks it unfinished.

## Waiting for a missing name

A missing name needs no record of what it waits for. An edit that declares a title changes the face of the loom it edits, the part of a module that other looms can see, so the server checks every open loom of the workspace again, as the chapter "The language server" describes. The anchor that waited then resolves at once, with no edit to its own loom. The composition asks for the dependents of a change over the new corpus as well as the old, so a loom whose anchor the edit has just satisfied is composed again too, as the chapter "The compile layer" describes.

The editor offers a fix that declares the missing section in the anchor's own loom: a heading at the level of the section that names it, placed just below that section.

## The book as a graph

The book's names and references, seen together, form a graph. Its nodes are the named things of the corpus: books, parts, chapters, discussions, glossaries and their terms, sections, boxes, and the files sections emit. Its edges are the references between them, of eight kinds:

- **Composes:** a `::[Name]` anchor in code, which draws the named section's code in place.
- **Refers:** a `::[Name]` anchor where the code expects a module path, which names a file the book emits, the way an import names a module.
- **Links:** a `::[Name]` anchor in prose, which a reader follows, including a reference to a term.
- **Places:** a table-of-contents entry, or a prose anchor that places a chapter in the book.
- **Emits:** a sink on a heading, from its section to the file the section writes.
- **Declares:** a box of kind `Chapter` or `Discussion`, from the box to the loom it points to.
- **Stands for:** a wildcard's `anchors` parameter, from the wildcard to the section or chapter that settles it.
- **Relates:** a parameter whose value is a reference, which a trope declares, from the section that carries the parameter to the section it names. The edge takes the parameter's key as its name, so the relations of one trope stay apart from those of every other, as the chapter "Tropes" describes.

Nothing builds this graph before compiling, and nothing in compiling reads it. Each piece already lives in the corpus. The `titleIndexes` and `chapterIndex` indexes give the nodes, and the sinks on the headings give the files. Each loom's product holds its composing edges. The anchors in its prose, its table of contents, and the reference parameters on its headings and boxes hold the rest. The graph is a projection of the produced corpus, a sibling of the compile, the tangle, and the weave, and it is computed only when something asks for it.

The corpus already treats the links between looms this way. The `anchorEdges` function derives them from `titleIndexes` and from every loom's anchors each time `reachable` or `dependentsOf` asks, and it keeps nothing between calls. The graph of the book derives its nodes and edges the same way, one level finer, with sections and files in place of whole looms.

This keeps the chain the book already settled: compiling resolves from the corpus, and no resolve pass stands between the two. Building the graph is cheap, a walk over each loom's headings and anchors, which costs far less than reading the looms' lines or type-checking their code. But a graph kept beside the corpus would be a second record of the same facts, and the language server would have to keep the two in step on every edit. A projection is never out of date, because it is read from the corpus it describes.

The graph serves the views that want the book as a whole: a drawing of which chapters compose which, the backlinks a woven page lists, or the looms a change would touch.

## What to work out

- **Titles that hold a comma.** A comma separates the names in a path, yet many titles hold a comma. Part X alone anchors `::[Once, as the site is built]`, `::[What the licence says, plainly]`, and `::[A chapter, cut into entries]`. So a name is first tried whole, and is split into a path only when the whole name matches nothing, which keeps those anchors resolving. How a path names a title with a comma inside it is still open.
- **How far a box's name reaches.** A bare name finds a box within the box's own chapter. Whether a box should also be found by a bare name from another chapter is open. A path reaches it either way. Wildcards press the question, since code in many chapters may share the need that one wildcard names.
- **Stubs in TypeScript 7.** TypeScript 7.0.2 ships none of the fixes that create code from its uses. Asked over its language server, it offers import fixes, "Implement interface", and the fixes for a missing type annotation. TypeScript 6.0.3 also adds a missing function, declares a missing method or property in another file, adds missing properties, implements an abstract class, and infers parameter types from usage. TypeScript 7's main branch holds the same code actions as 7.0.2, and its team does not plan to port every one. No version of TypeScript offers a fix for the case an unsettled section needs most: a name imported from a module that does not export it. So the trope that writes TypeScript's stubs writes them itself, from the types the language server reports at each use, or borrows TypeScript 6's fixes in a project that still runs TypeScript 6. Which of the two is open, and so is how a stub's types read when a use gives little to infer from, as when a value is only passed along.
- **Running before it is settled.** A wildcard's module could stand in for its target when a program runs, as it does in the editor, so a program runs before it is finished and fails only where it reaches a promised name. Whether a run asks for that, and what stands in for an unsettled section that an anchor composes, is open.
- **Where the graph is drawn.** Whether the first view of the graph is a `loom` verb, a panel in the editor, or a page of the website is open.

## What this chapter grows into

1. Wildcards: the `Wildcard` kind, its `anchors` parameter resolved, followed and renamed, and the module the editor serves while a wildcard is unsettled. They wait for boxes, and for the `Reference` kind of the chapter "Referring to modules", which marks a code anchor that refers.
2. The contract an unsettled section collects from the code that uses it and from the names anchors and wildcards promise, offered as completions and handed to the trope that writes stubs.
3. The `provides` names of a referring anchor, checked against the names its file exports.
4. The graph as a projection of the produced corpus, with its first view.

When anchors resolve by their place in the book and the editor follows them, this chapter joins Part II, The shape of a loom, beside "The multi-file AST".
